# p4pilot — sample Helix protections / permissions notes for a restricted agent user
#
# PURPOSE
#   Give studio admins a starting point for a bot identity that can open/edit
#   files for agent demos but CANNOT submit. Exact syntax and capability flags
#   vary by Helix Core version and site policy — treat every line as a template,
#   not a drop-in for production.
#
# SAFETY
#   - No real hostnames, tickets, or passwords.
#   - Do NOT paste this file into `p4 protect` without review by a Perforce admin.
#   - Test on a sandbox server first.
#
# HOW TO APPLY (admin runbook sketch — adapt to your site)
#   1. Create user  p4pilot-agent   (or your preferred bot name)
#   2. Create group ai-agents       (optional but recommended)
#   3. Add the bot user to the group
#   4. Merge adapted lines into the protections table (p4 protect)
#   5. Verify with VERIFY.md (safe read-only checks + controlled deny test)
#
# ILLUSTRATIVE NAMES ONLY
#   User:  p4pilot-agent
#   Group: ai-agents
#   Depot sandbox path example: //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
#   Human submitters keep separate accounts with normal write+submit rights.

# =============================================================================
# Recommended approach (summary)
# =============================================================================
#
# 1) Prefer a dedicated bot user + group over reusing human accounts.
# 2) Grant the bot only the depot paths the agent needs (sandbox first).
# 3) Deny submit server-side for that user/group (protections and/or triggers).
# 4) Keep human submit rights on a different user used in P4V after review.
# 5) Optionally add a change-submit trigger as a second layer (see triggers.sample).
#
# Protections alone may be enough on many sites. Triggers are a belt-and-suspenders
# option when you want an explicit, auditable "this user may never submit" gate.

# =============================================================================
# Example protections table fragments (COMMENTED — adapt before use)
# =============================================================================
#
# Protections are ordered; first matching line usually wins. Put more-specific
# denies/allows carefully relative to your existing table. The forms below are
# common patterns — confirm against `p4 help protect` for your server version.
#
# --- Group membership (separate from protect table) ---
#   p4 group ai-agents
#     Users:
#       p4pilot-agent
#
# --- Path-limited write for the bot group (sandbox) ---
# write group ai-agents * //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
#
# --- Broader read for review tools (optional) ---
# read  group ai-agents * //depot/...
#
# --- Explicit open/write without implying submit rights on some sites ---
# Some admins use `open` / `write` for edit rights and rely on a separate
# mechanism to block submit. Others use super/admin-only submit paths.
# Confirm which mode your studio already uses.
#
# open  user p4pilot-agent * //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
# write user p4pilot-agent * //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
#
# --- Explicit list/read for status and review ---
# list  user p4pilot-agent * //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
# read  user p4pilot-agent * //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
#
# --- What NOT to grant the bot ---
#   - super / admin
#   - unrestricted write on production game content paths
#   - any protection that effectively allows submit if your table equates
#     write with submit rights without a further gate
#
# If your site uses a "submitters" group that alone may submit, simply omit
# p4pilot-agent from that group and document that as the control.

# =============================================================================
# Permissions-style notes (when your site uses permissions, not only protect)
# =============================================================================
#
# Some deployments combine protections with permissions / ownership / stream
# ACLs / custom review gates. Whatever the mechanism, the contract for p4pilot
# demos is:
#
#   AS p4pilot-agent:
#     - may open/edit/add within the allowed sandbox path (if the demo needs it)
#     - must FAIL on submit (CLI and any GUI using that identity)
#   AS human-reviewer:
#     - may submit the pending changelist after review in P4V
#
# Document the chosen mechanism in your studio runbook; do not invent a second
# source of truth in application code.

# =============================================================================
# Cross-checks after edit
# =============================================================================
#
# Safe (non-destructive) checks — see VERIFY.md for the full checklist:
#   p4 -u p4pilot-agent info
#   p4 -u p4pilot-agent protects -m
#   p4 -u p4pilot-agent protects //depot/sandbox/agent-demo/...
#
# Controlled deny test (manual, not automated in CI):
#   Attempt submit as p4pilot-agent on a throwaway sandbox changelist and
#   confirm the server rejects it. Do this only on a non-production server
#   with admin approval. Never script production submits from this repo.
#
# Second layer: helix/triggers.sample and check-submit-deny.* scripts.
